# run a bad-block-aware fsck:
# Step 1: Unmount if partially mounted
sudo umount /dev/sdc1

# Step 2: Check with bad block detection (-c) and auto-repair (-y)
sudo e2fsck -f -c -y /dev/sdc1
The -c flag runs badblocks(8) to scan for physical bad sectors, writes them to the filesystem bad block inode, and prevents future allocations on them. After that, mount should work.
Step 3: Mount:
sudo mount /dev/sdc1 /mnt/disk4
